  "rounds": 1,
  "summary": {
    "title": "Add Ember & Oak fictional coffee shop landing page",
    "changes": [
      "Create a warm, single-page Next.js site with consistent Ember & Oak branding, locally hosted photography and fonts, an introduction, and shop story.",
      "Add eight menu items with USD prices, weekday and weekend opening hours, and the explicitly imaginary address at 24 Willow Lane, Bramblewick.",
      "Provide responsive desktop and mobile layouts, semantic headings, native section navigation, a skip link, visible keyboard focus, and reduced-motion support.",
      "Include a dependency lockfile, development and production scripts, documentation, and deterministic content and structure tests."
    ],
    "testing": [
      "All four tests, the production build, and TypeScript checks passed in local verification and were independently rerun successfully during review. The build statically prerendered the landing page.",
      "Browser QA passed all four acceptance criteria at 1280×800 and 390×844, checking content, branding, fictional disclosures, readability, navigation, and absence of observed horizontal overflow.",
      "Keyboard QA verified the visible skip link, desktop and mobile section navigation, and footer return link using Tab and Enter.",
      "Independent review started the production server, confirmed key page content and responsive viewport metadata, and received HTTP 200 responses for the font stylesheet, representative font files, and both photographs."
    ],
    "limitations": [
      "This is an informational fictional-shop site; ordering, payments, reservations, and backend functionality are intentionally absent.",
      "Independent review could not launch a browser because Chrome was unavailable in its environment. Its browser-usability assessment relied on supplied desktop/mobile screenshots, page snapshots, and keyboard evidence; separate browser QA passed.",
      "Automated tests cover content and structure; CSS-presence assertions alone do not establish browser usability."
    ]
  },
